    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### f-strings  \n",
        "I will use [f-strings](https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/python/formatted-string-literals-f-strings-python/) often in this course to **print information** or **construct file-paths**. The basic construction of an f-string is as follows:  \n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "```\n",
        "block_count = 50\n",
        "print(f'The variable I defined is equal to {block_count}')\n",
        "```\n",
        "The printed output would then be:\n",
        "```\n",
        "The variable I defined is equal to 50\n",
        "```\n",
        "The main features of the f-string are that it starts with an f directly before the string (no space), and any variables you want to print out or add to your string should be included in curly braces {} within the string.  \n",
        "Below I am defining the data-path where the files that we will use are held. I will later use a lot of f-strings that begin with {dpath} to short-cut in this directory.\n",
        "\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "ySa156H-YHCC"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "dpath = '/content/MSE7530/sampledata/plastics/known/'"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "r4lVKbt5n9oD"
      },
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Pandas  \n",
        "We will use Pandas often in this class. Its main strength is the two-dimensional data structure called a **DataFrame**, which is a table with rows and columns. The particularly useful functions we will use a lot is \"read_csv\", \"read_excel\", etc., which load a file into your notebook and create a dataframe.  Rather than have me reinvent the wheel in this notebook, here are a few resources you can use to become familiar with dataframes:  \n",
        "[Pandas in 10 minutes](https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/10min.html)  \n",
        "[Getting Started](https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/getting_started/index.html)  \n",
        "[User Guide](https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/index.html#user-guide)  \n",
        "Click around these websites to learn more - there are a lot of user guides and tutorials.\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "V78swmEuoSgL"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Part 1. Manual identification of functional groups.  \n",
        "In the first part, you will use the assignment tables given in class to predict the locations of the stretching peaks for your assigned polymer. You will use this as your physical reference when you are working with the automation later. Use a markup cell to type out the peaks, and then use a code cell to create a list of the locations where you would expect the peaks."
