    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Part 2. Find peaks"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "c4JaiNBMkfXb"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "from scipy.signal import find_peaks"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "0atPZ8rxGX-L"
      },
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "result = find_peaks(pol3['Abs'])\n",
        "result"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "O2b3JqdEhRnL"
      },
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "So what does this mean? How do we turn this into anything physical? To begin, we ran find_peaks on **just** the absorbance data. It treats this as 1-dimensional data; it only looks at each point's value in comparison to its neighbors on the array. It returns the **index**, or position, of the numbers in the array that it determines are peaks.  \n",
        "In order to turn this into useful information, we need to do 2 things:  \n",
        "1. Take only the first item in the tuple, which is the numpy array of all peak indices.  \n",
        "2. Apply the indices to **both** the wavenumbers and the absorbance values."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "jxc5mt-AGuQi"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "pk_inds = result[0] # necessary to grab peak indices\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n",
        "ax.plot(pol3['wavenumber'], pol3['Abs'], label='data') # plot the data we have from above\n",
        "ax.plot(pol3['wavenumber'][pk_inds], pol3['Abs'][pk_inds], 'x', label='peaks') # plot the peaks\n",
        "ax.legend()\n",
        "ax.set_xlabel('Wavenumber (cm$^{-1}$)')\n",
        "ax.set_ylabel('Absorbance')\n",
        "ax.set_xlim(4000,400) # modify this if you want to zoom in to something\n",
        "for e in expected_pks: # this plots where we expected to find the peaks\n",
        "  if type(e) == tuple:\n",
        "    ax.axvspan(e[0],e[1],color='grey',alpha=0.5)\n",
        "  elif type(e) == float or type(e) == int:\n",
        "    ax.axvline(x=e,color='k',linestyle='--',linewidth=1)"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "hsU53R2iLss-"
      },
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "As described in the class slides (and online documentation), there are multiple **arguments** you can pass to find_peaks that will change what it determines is a peak - we will focus on height, threshold, distance, and prominence"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "W1bf84i9I2Yf"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "result2 = find_peaks(pol3['Abs'],height=-2.)\n",
        "result2"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "FcMJKAijhXVb"
      },
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "pk_inds = result2[0] # necessary to grab peak indices\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n",
        "ax.plot(pol3['wavenumber'], pol3['Abs'], label='data') # plot the data we have from above\n",
        "ax.plot(pol3['wavenumber'][pk_inds], pol3['Abs'][pk_inds], 'x', label='peaks') # plot the peaks\n",
        "ax.legend()\n",
        "ax.set_xlabel('Wavenumber (cm$^{-1}$)')\n",
        "ax.set_ylabel('Absorbance')\n",
        "ax.set_xlim(4000,400) # modify this if you want to zoom in to something"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "PBnWhKKOkCAY"
      },
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
